      "sign": "Juan-E.Mart\u00ednez-Legaz (Barcelona)"
     },
     "text": "Let \\(A\\) and \\(C\\) be convex and compact subsets of \\(\\mathbb{R}^d\\). One says that \\(C\\) rolls freely in \\(A\\) if for each boundary point \\(a\\in \\partial A\\) and each rotation \\(\\rho\\) there exists \\(x\\in\\mathbb{R}^d\\) such that \\(a\\in x+\\rho (C) \\subset A\\). If \\(A\\) is only closed, one requires in addition that \\((A^c+(-1)C)^c\\) be path-connected, where \\(^c\\) and \\(+\\) denote complement and Minkowski addition, respectively, and \\((-1)C:= \\{-c:c\\in C\\}\\).\n\n\nThe main result in this paper states that a ball of radius \\(r\\) rolls freely inside a nonempty, compact and path-connected set \\(S\\) and inside the \\(S^c\\), the closure of \\(\\overline{S^c}\\), for all \\(0\\leq r\\leq r_0\\) if and only if \\(S\\) and \\(\\overline{S^c}\\) are intersections of complements of open balls with radius \\(r_0\\) and \\(S\\) has a nonempty interior. Another equivalent condition is \\(S\\) to belong to the regular model of \\textit{J. Serra} [Image analysis and mathematical morphology. Academic Press (1982; Zbl 0565.92001)]. Applications to the analysis and smoothing of images are discussed.",
     "contribution_type": "review"
    }
   ],
